From 5dbd63dc86ccc53faefa55b4fb25fd9b9f2c7f82 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hasan Mehdi <hasanthemehdi@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 20:01:02 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] bt: 48 kHz to the headphones, and a sound quality setting
 (SBC dual channel)

Every link ran at 44.1 kHz although the music is 48 kHz Opus and the Galaxy Buds3 Pro
take 48: ESP-IDF's source offered 44.1 only, so Rockbox's linear resampler (treble -3 dB
at 15 kHz, plus aliasing) sat in front of every song, and the app's 48 kHz preference was
refused ("pref mcc status 6" = unsupported). Its encoder also aimed at a fixed 328 kbps,
bitpool 53 for 44.1 kHz joint stereo only.

The stack now builds the config for each sink from what it says it takes
(bta_av_co_sbc_cfg_for_sink): 48 kHz when it can, joint stereo up to bitpool 53, or in the
high setting dual channel up to bitpool 38 per channel ("SBC XQ", about 492 kbps at 48 kHz);
16 blocks, 8 subbands, loudness. That config is also the one the encoder is set up from.
The encoder aims at the top of the agreed bitpool (the lowest rate whose bitpool reaches
it; basic rate links still no more than 229 kbps) and packs one media packet per radio
packet, as Android does (663 bytes on 2 Mbit EDR, so a packet no longer goes out as two).
A reconfigured stream reports its config to the app like the first one does.

bluetooth.c: bt_set_quality (remembered in bt_devices.txt as quality=); while streaming
the stream stops and starts again, and the link is reconfigured on the way. bt_status
gains hq, link_hz and link_kbps; bt.log says "configured sbc 48000 Hz dual channel,
bitpool 2-38 (492 kbps)". The preferred-config call is gone.

Host test (Bluedroid's encoder, ffmpeg's SBC decoder, three songs from the card): error
vs the file, 12-16 kHz, metal: today -9 dB, 48 kHz joint -13, dual channel -24; overall
SNR 28.5 / 32.0 / 41.8 dB.

